;; M1e2 S1e2.2.4 — Q-M1e.21's residue, refused at load.
;;
;; `mirror` names the relation it matches and asserts by its parameter ?R,
;; and the activator binds it: `(mirror q)` would assert `(q B A)`. `(mirror 3)`
;; binds it to an integer, whose decimal text the compiler would intern as a
;; relation's name — a name the program does not have, which no rule's text
;; predicts and so no candidate can hold. The user ruled on 2026-10-16 that
;; rules cannot assert new names (Q-M1e.21 (b)); every other name a rule can
;; introduce is a constant it writes, which the candidates are seeded with
;; since S1e2.2.4, so this is the one refused.
;;
;; The loader refuses it where the activator is written down: here, a fact.
;; An activator a rule derives through a variable is the compiler's to refuse
;; (`ein-infer/tests/compile_semantics.rs`).
(relation q T T)

(q A B)
(mirror 3)

(rule mirror (?R)
  :match  (?R ?a ?b)
  :assert (?R ?b ?a))
